@ [dual_hemisphere] C:\Project\Cantor\narrative\research\Cantor_Runtime_Procedure_FPGA_Dual_Hemisphere_Review_2026-08-05.sop

& [Claim_CDRA_01] an in-memory reference runtime is the correct next proof surface
  + [support] pure CTPR forms and validation are implemented while runtime evidence is absent
  + [support] in-memory explicit state removes persistence, service, concurrency, and provider variables from semantic proof
  + [counterargument] a production database would reveal more realistic problems
  + [answer] production concerns are real but would confound whether the state transitions themselves are coherent and replayable
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] interpreter semantics require an external transaction property that cannot be represented by explicit state

& [Claim_CDRA_02] logical time must precede live time
  + [support] Calendar, wake, recurrence, timeout, and replay need exact deterministic ordering
  + [counterargument] real calendars ultimately depend on wall time and timezone libraries
  + [answer] fixture civil time and explicit timezone policy can be supplied as data; providers and clocks remain later adapters
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] a required first-proof behavior cannot be expressed from supplied temporal data

& [Claim_CDRA_03] repository generations and compare-and-append are the correct transition waist
  + [support] they expose stale state, branch, merge, event frontier, snapshot, and index authority explicitly
  + [counterargument] ordinary mutable collections are simpler
  + [answer] mutable collections obscure predecessor identity and make replay and stale-write proofs weaker
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] immutable generation overhead prevents even bounded fixture evaluation without adding audit value

& [Claim_CDRA_04] Planner must return proposals rather than commitments
  + [support] planning quality, calendar commitment, consent, authority, and execution are separate meanings
  + [counterargument] a task planner that cannot schedule or execute is less useful
  + [answer] the first runtime proves decisions before effect adapters; later profiles may add separately authorized transitions
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] downstream commitment cannot consume a versioned proposal without ambiguous reconstruction

& [Claim_CDRA_05] tandem lanes need messages and joins instead of shared mutable state
  + [support] exact messages preserve participant, version, causality, acknowledgment, and residuals
  + [counterargument] shared state is faster and easier inside one process
  + [answer] performance is not yet measured, while explicit messages are essential to replay and eventual cross-process coordination
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] deterministic replay cannot be achieved without hidden shared state

& [Claim_CDRA_06] timeouts and reentry should be logical state transitions
  + [support] sleeps, timers, and leases introduce ambient scheduling and nondeterminism
  + [counterargument] real systems require actual timeouts
  + [answer] real timeout adapters can later emit explicit operations; core semantics still evaluate supplied transitions
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] no adapter boundary can faithfully translate live timeout observations into the core form

& [Claim_CDRA_07] a bounded compiler-diff fixture is useful without a general compiler claim
  + [support] it tests forward prediction, independent rear correspondence, diff completeness, invalidation, and proof semantics
  + [counterargument] fixed data may only test itself
  + [answer] multiple positive and adversarial fixtures plus generative transition tests can prove the protocol while syntax and backend remain separate
  + [confidence] is medium_high
  + [invalidation] fixture forms cannot express a later real compiler's relevant correspondence edges

& [Claim_CDRA_08] byte-stable normalized replay is the primary first-proof criterion
  + [support] it directly detects ambient order, time, identity, and serialization drift
  + [counterargument] byte equality may overconstrain harmless implementation variation
  + [answer] equality applies to canonical semantic results and traces, not internal memory layout or performance telemetry
  + [confidence] is high
  + [invalidation] a semantically relevant output cannot be normalized without loss

& [JustificationDisposition] is accepted_for_canonical_authoring
  + eight claims survive their strongest current counterarguments
  + live effects, persistence, concurrency, and provider integration remain deferred
